orthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n1529,CENTRAL COMPOSITE DESIGN,\"**Central Composite Design (CCD) Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nCentral Composite Design (CCD) is a statistical technique employed in response surface methodology (RSM) to construct a second-order (quadratic) model for the response variable. It is particularly useful for optimizing responses by varying multiple factors, allowing researchers to estimate the curvature of the response surface without the need for a full three-level factorial experiment.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe general form of the second-order polynomial model used in CCD can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_{ii} X_i^2 + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( X_i \\) are the factors, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients, \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients, \\( \\beta_{ii} \\) are the quadratic coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nA Central Composite Design consists of:\n- A factorial or fractional factorial design (which provides the corner points of the design space).\n- Center points (which help in estimating experimental error and checking for curvature).\n- Axial points (or star points) that are positioned at a distance \\( \\alpha \\) from the center along each axis, allowing for the estimation of curvature.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of CCD relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately quadratic.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n3. Homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors) is assumed across the range of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nCCD is applicable when:\n- The researcher aims to optimize a response influenced by multiple factors.\n- A second-order model is anticipated to adequately describe the response surface.\n- There is a need to explore the effects of factors and their interactions efficiently without conducting a full factorial experiment.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data obtained from a CCD typically involves:\n1. Fitting the second-order polynomial model using regression analysis.\n2. Conducting ANOVA to assess the significance of the model and individual factors.\n3. Performing diagnostic checks for model assumptions.\n4. Using response surface plots to visualize the effects and identify optimal conditions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- CCD is efficient in terms of the number of runs required compared to full factorial designs.\n- It allows for the estimation of curvature, which is essential for understanding the response surface.\n- The design can accommodate blocking, making it versatile for various experimental conditions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- CCD may not be suitable if the true relationship is not quadratic.\n- The design can become complex with an increasing number of factors, leading to a larger number of required runs.\n- The choice of the axial distance \\( \\alpha \\) can influence the design's efficiency and the accuracy of the curvature estimation.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include Box-Behnken designs and full factorial designs. Alternatives to CCD may involve using D-optimal designs or other RSM techniques depending on the specific experimental context and objectives.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing CCD, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate levels for factors based on prior knowledge or exploratory studies.\n- The need for randomization to mitigate bias.\n- The potential for interaction effects and the importance of including them in the model.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide built-in functions for designing and analyzing CCD experiments, facilitating the implementation of this methodology.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCCD is widely used in various fields, including chemical engineering for optimizing reaction conditions, agriculture for improving crop yields, and pharmaceuticals for formulating drug products. It is particularly valuable in industrial applications where multiple factors influence product quality and performance.\",15\r\n2406,FACTORIAL PORTION,,1\r\n2404,AXIAL POINTS,Axial points are specific points in the design that are used to explore the response surface beyond the factorial points.,1\r\n2421,INSCRIBED CCD,Inscribed central composite design refers to a central composite design that fits within a specified design space.,1\r\n2547,CHROMATOGRAPHIC EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION,\"Chromatographic exponential function is a response measured in the optimization of the production of ranitidine, which should be minimized.\",1\r\n1521,CENTER POINTS,\"**CENTER POINTS**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: Center points are additional experimental runs incorporated into a design to provide an estimate of experimental error and to assess curvature in the response surface. They serve as a critical component in response surface methodology (RSM) and are particularly useful in factorial designs, where they help to evaluate the linearity of the response function and detect any non-linear effects.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**: In the context of a factorial design, center points are typically located at the midpoint of the range of each factor. For a two-factor design, if the factors are denoted as \\(X_1\\) and \\(X_2\\), the center point can be represented as \\((\\bar{X_1}, \\bar{X_2})\\), where \\(\\bar{X_1}\\) and \\(\\bar{X_2}\\) are the average levels of factors \\(X_1\\) and \\(X_2\\), respectively. The inclusion of center points allows for the estimation of curvature in the response surface model, which can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\beta_{11} X_1^2 + \\beta_{22} X_2^2 + \\beta_{12} X_1 X_2 + \\epsilon\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Y\\) is the response variable, \\(\\beta\\) coefficients represent the effects, and \\(\\epsilon\\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**: Center points are typically added to the existing factorial or fractional factorial design. For example, in a \\(2^k\\) factorial design, center points can be added at the center of the cube defined by the factor levels. The design matrix will include these additional runs, which are repeated to provide a reliable estimate of experimental error.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The validity of center points relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the factors and the response is smooth and continuous.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and homoscedastic (constant variance).\n3. The experimental runs are independent.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: Center points are particularly applicable in situations where:\n- The researcher suspects non-linearity in the response surface.\n- There is a need for a robust estimate of experimental error.\n- The design involves multiple factors, and the interaction effects are of interest.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: The analysis of data including center points typically involves fitting a response surface model using regression techniques. The significance of curvature can be assessed through hypothesis testing on the quadratic terms in the model. ANOVA can be employed to evaluate the overall model fit and the significance of individual effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:\n- Center points enhance the ability to detect curvature in the response surface, leading to more accurate modeling.\n- They provide an independent estimate of experimental error, improving the reliability of statistical inferences.\n- The inclusion of center points can increase the robustness of the design against model misspecification.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:\n- The addition of center points increases the total number of experimental runs, which may not be feasible in resource-limited situations.\n- If the response surface is truly linear, the inclusion of center points may not provide significant additional information.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: Center points are commonly used in conjunction with factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and central composite designs (CCD). Alternatives to center points include using a full central composite design, which systematically incorporates center points along with axial points to enhance the exploration of the response surface.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When implementing center points, researchers should consider the number of center points to include, as multiple replicates can provide a more reliable estimate of error. The placement of center points should be carefully planned to ensure they are representative of the experimental space.\n\n**Software Implementation**: Many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide built-in functions for designing experiments that include center points. These tools facilitate the generation of design matrices and the analysis of variance for models incorporating center points.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: Center points are widely used in industrial experimentation, agricultural studies, and any field where response surface methodology is applied. For instance, in optimizing chemical processes, center points can help identify the optimal conditions for yield by assessing the curvature of the response surface.\",5\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3317,CENTRAL COMPOSITE DESIGN,BLOCK 1,\"Block 1 is a component of the Central Composite Design, consisting of factorial points and center poin